# Python bytecode (never track — caused recurring per-cycle noise, C3864)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Stale/backup checkpoint snapshots
*.bak
*.stale-*

# Local fetch caches — raw QPU result rows re-fetchable from the job IDs in the manifests.
# Bulky (n=8 C1 alone is 551 KB / 419,904 rows) and derived, so it is repo noise, not a record.
# Rule requested by Elder + authorized by Ember (general#2380/#2381): the point is that it is
# belt-AND-suspenders. Explicit-path staging is the real discipline (Ember C4196: a `git add -A`
# swept siblings' uncommitted work in THIS repo), but a .gitignore protects everyone from anyone's
# bad day, including a future DC who does not know the convention.
results/cache/

# Auth/credential operational notes — contain account identifiers, keep local only
NEW-INSTANCE-AUTH.md
demo/casebook/img/_*.png
results/exp_hss_race_SEALED_REVEAL_DO_NOT_COMMIT.json

# C4273 (Ember) — RAW, ARM-LABELLED FLIGHT DUMPS MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED.
# The cell-2 dump (results/h13_cell2_refly_RAWDUMP_<job>.json) was tracked and pushed to
# origin/main with keys pairing content to arm ("0|CE", "0|CC"), which made the DEBLINDING TABLE
# public: any reader could content-match a blinded export against it and recover all 80 arms.
# That single file falsified the reader-blind half of the claim we were about to publish.
#
# It cannot be un-published — `git rm` leaves it retrievable from history (4015f7d0727d), and a
# history rewrite would force-push a repo three seats work in and still miss any earlier clone.
# So the CLAIM was narrowed instead. This rule exists so that dataset is the LAST one with the
# property rather than the pattern: publish the JOB ID for provenance, never the table.
results/*RAWDUMP*.json
results/*rawdump*.json

# Blind-protocol hygiene (C5058, channels 9+10): RAW arm-labelled dumps are DEBLINDING TABLES.
# Committing one publishes the mapping to every reader of the repo, and `git rm` does not undo it
# (the blob stays reachable at its commit). Never commit these; they are the sealer working input.
results/*RAWDUMP*.json

# ── C5060: THE C5058 RULE WAS WRITTEN AGAINST THE FILE THAT LEAKED, NOT THE CLASS ──────────────
# `results/*RAWDUMP*.json` matches ONLY a top-level .json, because a gitignore `*` does not cross
# a `/`. Tested, not assumed — three realistic variants slipped straight through:
#     results/somejob/…RAWDUMP….json    -> WOULD BE COMMITTED   (and Cell 2 data ALREADY lands
#     results/nested/deep/RAWDUMP_x.json -> WOULD BE COMMITTED    in per-job SUBDIRECTORIES)
#     results/…RAWDUMP….jsonl            -> WOULD BE COMMITTED   (extension change defeats it)
# A deblinding table pairs CONTENT with ARM; whoever holds it can deblind any export by
# content-matching, and `git rm` does not undo a push. The rule must bind the CLASS at any depth
# and any extension. Found by checking my own repo after Ember reported a file that was true on
# disk and false in the repo — a narrow rule LOOKS like protection, which is why it survived.
**/*RAWDUMP*
**/*rawdump*
**/*DEBLIND*
**/*deblind*
